 "arms": {
  "full": {
   "text": "**Essay:**\n\nBenjamin Franklin was a multifaceted American who made significant contributions to science, politics, and culture. Born in 1706 in Boston, Massachusetts, he was the son of a printer and a bookseller. From a young age, Franklin displayed a keen intellect and a love for learning. He was known for his wit, humor, and his ability to think critically.\n\nFranklin was a founding father of the United States, playing a key role in the drafting of the Declaration of Independence and the Constitution. He was also a prominent figure in the American Revolution, advocating for independence and representing the colonies in the Continental Congress.\n\nIn the realm of science, Franklin made groundbreaking discoveries, including the concept of electricity, the lightning rod, and the invention of the lightning rod. He was also a pioneer in the field of science and education, establishing the first public library in America and promoting the importance of education.\n\nFranklin was a man of many talents, including being a writer, a printer, an inventor, and a philosopher. His life was a testament to the power of perseverance, curiosity, and the pursuit of knowledge. He was a symbol of the Enlightenment and a beacon of American ideals.\n\n****** \n\n**Poem:**\n\nBenjamin Franklin, born in Boston,  \nA mind that sparkled with bright light.  \nFrom printer’s son to statesman’s throne,  \nHe shaped the future with his own hand.\n\nHe discovered electricity,  \nA spark that lit the dark and cold.  \nThe lightning rod, a marvel of science,  \nA symbol of courage and bold.\n\nHe wrote the Declaration,  \nA voice for freedom and right.  \nIn the halls of Congress, he stood,  \nA leader of the revolution’s fight.\n\nA man of many talents,  \nA philosopher, a writer, a man.  \nHis life was a tale of wisdom,  \nA beacon for all who dare to dream.\n\nFrom ink to ink, he wrote the truth,  \nA legacy that will never die.  \nBenjamin Franklin, a true hero,  \nA man who changed the world.  \n\nreflecting on his legacy of innovation and diplomacy.",
